<h2>Why maintenance never truly closes</h2>
<p>The architecture of Sections 144 to 147 BNSS is built for revisiting. Section 146 allows alteration of an existing order on proof of a change in circumstances; Section 144(5) permits cancellation on specified grounds; the interim machinery operates while the main petition is pending; and the entire jurisdiction is a summary one, designed to prevent destitution rather than conclusively adjudicate civil rights. The civil-court doctrines of finality were made for decrees that settle title; a maintenance order settles this month&rsquo;s subsistence. That is why the ending of a first petition rarely bars a second claim outright — and also why the second claim must be honest about the first, because the one thing that reliably sinks it is suppression.</p>

<h2>Five endings, five exits</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>How round one ended</th><th>What it decided</th><th>The route back</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Dismissed in default (claimant absent)</td><td>Nothing on merits</td><td>Prompt restoration application before the same court; failing that, a fresh petition</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Withdrawn — typically on reconciliation</td><td>Nothing; the cause was given up, not decided</td><td>Fresh petition on the fresh cause: the renewed separation and present inability</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Dismissed on merits</td><td>The issues actually decided, on that record</td><td>Revision against the findings; or a fresh petition pleading changed circumstances</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Interim refused, case pending</td><td>Only the prima facie view</td><td>Press the main petition to final adjudication; renew interim on new material</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Order passed, then cancelled under S.146/S.144(5)</td><td>That the grounds for cancellation existed then</td><td>Fresh application if the disqualifying state of affairs has itself changed</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The default dismissal: decide fast, move faster</h2>
<p>Most second-petition problems begin as neglected first-petition problems. A petition dismissed because the claimant stopped appearing — commonly during a patch-up engineered by elders — should be addressed at once, because the choice between restoration and refiling is largely a function of time. A restoration sought within weeks, supported by an affidavit explaining the absence, is usually granted and preserves the original filing date, which matters because maintenance is ordinarily reckoned with the date of application in view. A refiling years later starts the clock afresh and surrenders the interregnum. Counsel taking over such a matter should obtain the complete order sheet of the first round before drafting anything: the exits differ depending on whether the dismissal order recorded anything beyond the absence.</p>

<h2>The merits dismissal: what is truly closed</h2>
<p>A dismissal after evidence occupies different ground. The welfare character of the jurisdiction does not license endless identical attempts; what it licenses is a fresh look when the facts are fresh. The working distinction is between <em>status findings</em> and <em>circumstance findings</em>. A finding that no marriage existed, or that the respondent is not the father, goes to status: it will confront the claimant in every later round until displaced in revision, and the second petition is not the forum to displace it. A finding that the claimant was earning adequately, or that the respondent&rsquo;s means were modest, goes to circumstances: it was true of a moment, and a petition pleading the lost job, the illness, the respondent&rsquo;s visible new prosperity, raises a new question that no earlier order answered. The drafting consequence is direct — a second petition should be built expressly on what has changed, with the first round disclosed in its opening paragraphs and distinguished, not buried.</p>

<h2>Withdrawal and the reconciliation cycle</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Record the terms of any reconciliation.</strong> If the first petition is being withdrawn because the parties are resuming cohabitation, a short statement recording that the withdrawal is on that basis protects the right to return if the resumption fails.</li>
  <li><strong>Withdrawal is not waiver.</strong> Giving up a pending petition does not extinguish the statutory right to maintenance, which springs from the relationship and present need — a fresh separation generates a fresh cause.</li>
  <li><strong>Mind the parallel proceedings.</strong> Where claims were also pending under the DV Act or Section 24 HMA, each should be dealt with expressly at the time of settlement or withdrawal; stray live proceedings surface awkwardly in round two.</li>
  <li><strong>Interim arrears need attention before withdrawal.</strong> Amounts already fallen due under an interim order should be paid or expressly addressed; the one-year execution window under Section 144(3) continues to run regardless of the reconciliation.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Candour as strategy</h2>
<p>Every second petition is read against the first, and respondents can be relied upon to place the earlier record before the court on the first date. The claimant who discloses the prior round, annexes its orders, and explains the sequence controls that narrative; the claimant who is silent hands the respondent an opening submission about suppression that colours everything after it. The same discipline binds respondents: resisting a restored or fresh petition by pretending the first dismissal decided the merits, when the order sheet shows a default, is the mirror-image credibility error. In a jurisdiction where so much turns on affidavits and judicial impressions of candour, the party who is straightforward about the unflattering history usually ends up ahead of the party who edited it.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Before advising on a second round, assemble three documents: the first petition, the order that ended it, and the complete order sheet. The ending order controls the exit; the order sheet reveals concessions and recorded statements that the parties themselves have forgotten; and the first petition fixes what was pleaded then — which is the baseline against which the &ldquo;change in circumstances&rdquo; of the new petition will be measured.</p>
</div>
